.TH GXWI-UPGRADE-PEIOS 1 "2026-10-06" "Peios"
.SH NAME
gxwi-upgrade-peios \- move this machine to the next release of Peios, on a GXWI desktop
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B gxwi-upgrade-peios
.SH DESCRIPTION
Upgrade Peios is the desktop's window on upgrade-peios. It has no authority
of its own: it runs upgrade-peios as the person, so an upgrade made here is
exactly the one the command makes. It never parses what either tool prints
for a person, only their JSON.
.PP
At the top is the release this machine runs, and its edition package with
that package's version. Upgrade Peios looks for a newer release as it opens,
and again with Check Now: it refreshes the repositories this machine already
trusts, with peipkg, then asks upgrade-peios what upgrading would change,
changing nothing. When a newer release is offered, every change is shown,
with what it downloads and takes once installed.
.PP
Upgrade to... makes it. Apply Its Settings at the Next Restart holds the
release's settings, its registry seeds, until the next restart instead of
applying them once it is installed. If the changes differ by the time it
starts, they are shown and asked about again, and anything peipkg needs
the person's own approval for is asked on its own. The page then shows how
far it has got. Once approved, the upgrade is made to the end even if the
window is closed: it stays, saying so, until the upgrade is done. The
machine is not restarted.
.PP
Where a repository's information is out of date, Continue Anyway carries on
with what this machine has, as upgrade-peios \-\-allow-stale does. Where the
release's settings are waiting for the next restart, Apply Now applies them
at once, as upgrade-peios \-\-seeds-only does.
.PP
Whether the person may upgrade is asked of the system, by upgrade-peios. As
shipped, only Administrators may. Anyone else sees the release, with the
reason said once, and nothing to press. Software other than Peios itself is
updated in Package Manager.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /usr/share/apps/dev.peios.gxwi-upgrade-peios.toml
Its application declaration, listed in a launcher as Upgrade Peios.
.TP
.I /usr/bin/upgrade-peios
What it runs, to read the release and to make the upgrade.
.TP
.I /usr/bin/peipkg
What it runs to list and refresh the trusted repositories.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR peipkg (1).
Upgrade Peios, and Upgrading Peios, at learn.peios.org.
